course: dictionary
lesson: 5104
title: The internet
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The internet is a huge worldwide network. It links millions of computers, phones and other devices so they can share information, send messages and show websites and videos. Think of it as a web of connections that reaches almost everywhere.
explain: «The internet began in 1969 by linking a few computers in American universities and grew into a worldwide network.» The word grew tells you it started small and got bigger over time. Network is the key idea: many things joined together so they can talk to each other.
explain: The World Wide Web is a set of web pages on the internet. The internet is the whole network, and the web is one thing you can use on it. Email, video calls and games travel across the internet too.
explain: «When Ruby types a web address, a system called DNS, the internet's phone book, turns the name into an IP address number.» Context helps here: a phone book links names to numbers, so DNS does the same job for websites. Names are for people, numbers are for computers.
fact: The internet is a huge worldwide network that links millions of computers, phones and other devices.
fact: The World Wide Web is the set of web pages you can look at on the internet.
fact: A network is many things joined together so they can share information.
fact: Never share your full name, address, school, passwords or photos with strangers online.
fact: Not everything on the internet is true, so check facts with trusted sources.
exercise: What is the internet? | *A huge worldwide network that links computers, phones and other devices | A single website about animals | A game you play on a tablet
exercise: «The internet also has tricksters.» What is a trickster? | *Someone who tries to fool people | A type of computer cable | A helpful teacher
exercise: «Names are for people, numbers are for computers.» Who are numbers for, according to this sentence? | *Computers | Phone calls only | Pets | People
test: Which is the best safety rule online? | *Never share your passwords or photos with strangers | Share your password with your best friend | Give your full address to new friends | Tell everyone your school name
test: A chatbot gave silly advice, such as putting glue on pizza, after picking up a joke from the internet. What does this show? | The internet is always right | *Not everything on the internet is true or sensible | Jokes are never found online | Chatbots never make mistakes
exam: If something you see online upsets you, what should you do? | *Tell a trusted adult | Share it with all your friends | Reply angrily to the person who posted it | Keep it secret and say nothing
